160: /**
161:  * A {@link SchedulerNG} implementation that uses the declarative resource management and
162:  * automatically adapts the parallelism in case not enough resource could be acquired to run at the
163:  * configured parallelism, as described in FLIP-160.
164:  *
165:  * <p>This scheduler only supports jobs with streaming semantics, i.e., all vertices are connected
166:  * via pipelined data-exchanges.
167:  *
168:  * <p>The implementation is spread over multiple {@link State} classes that control which RPCs are
169:  * allowed in a given state and what state transitions are possible (see the FLIP for an overview).
170:  * This class can thus be roughly split into 2 parts:
171:  *
172:  * <p>1) RPCs, which must forward the call to the state via {@link State#tryRun(Class,
173:  * ThrowingConsumer, String)} or {@link State#tryCall(Class, FunctionWithException, String)}.
174:  *
175:  * <p>2) Context methods, which are called by states, to either transition into another state or
176:  * access functionality of some component in the scheduler.
177:  */
178: public class AdaptiveScheduler
179:         implements SchedulerNG,
180:                 Created.Context,
181:                 WaitingForResources.Context,
182:                 CreatingExecutionGraph.Context,
183:                 Executing.Context,
184:                 Restarting.Context,
185:                 Failing.Context,
186:                 Finished.Context,
187:                 StopWithSavepoint.Context {
